WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.544
The Indexing module turns content items and other records into documents for a search index.

00:00:06.544 --> 00:00:10.448
In this video, you write a part handler, a field handler, and a

00:00:10.448 --> 00:00:13.050
source for records that are not content.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.556
An index profile has a provider, such as Lucene, and a type.

00:00:21.556 --> 00:00:24.692
The type names the source that feeds the index.

00:00:24.692 --> 00:00:29.190
The built-in type is Content, and each built-in provider registers it.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:35.198
When a content item is created, updated, published or removed, the Indexing module

00:00:35.198 --> 00:00:38.364
records a task for it, an update or a deletion.

00:00:38.364 --> 00:00:42.427
At the end of the request, it builds the document and sends it

00:00:42.427 --> 00:00:44.100
to each content index.

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:50.134
The Indexing Worker feature adds a background task, Indexes Content Updater.

00:00:50.134 --> 00:00:55.226
Every minute, it reads the task log from the last position of each index.

00:00:55.226 --> 00:01:01.670
The request and the worker build the document with the same I Document Index Handler services.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:11.096
To index a part, derive from Content Part Index Handler and name the part type.

00:01:11.096 --> 00:01:14.539
Build Index Async receives the part and a context.

00:01:14.539 --> 00:01:17.490
This is the handler of the Html Body part.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.196
The context lists the keys to index a value under:

00:01:21.196 --> 00:01:25.048
the part name, and the content type name followed by the part name.

00:01:25.048 --> 00:01:27.740
The handler sets the value once for each key.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:31.484
The options come from the settings of the provider.

00:01:31.484 --> 00:01:35.948
For Lucene, the Keyword and Stored settings become the Keyword and Store flags.

00:01:35.948 --> 00:01:39.310
A handler can ignore them, as the Alias part handler does.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.391
Register the handler as an I Content Part Index Handler.

00:01:43.391 --> 00:01:47.527
The Content Item Index Coordinator skips a part that is not included in

00:01:47.527 --> 00:01:52.040
the settings of the provider, unless the part is named after the content type.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.816
A field handler follows the same pattern.

00:01:58.816 --> 00:02:02.651
Derive from Content Field Index Handler and name the field type.

00:02:02.651 --> 00:02:07.090
This is the handler of the Text field, and it sets the text under each key.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.896
The context also gives you the field definition and its settings.

00:02:11.896 --> 00:02:16.466
The Numeric field handler reads the scale, and indexes an integer when the

00:02:16.466 --> 00:02:18.980
scale is zero, and a number otherwise.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:22.790
Register it as an I Content Field Index Handler.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:26.813
A field is indexed only when its own settings include it, and its

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:30.100
part is included or is named after the content type.

00:02:34.383 --> 00:02:37.341
Whether an element is indexed is a setting.

00:02:37.341 --> 00:02:42.652
Under Design, Content Definition, Content Types, edit the Blog Post type, and then

00:02:42.652 --> 00:02:44.463
edit its Subtitle field.

00:02:44.928 --> 00:02:48.739
The Subtitle settings page has a Lucene Index settings section.

00:02:48.739 --> 00:02:52.882
Include this element in the index lets the Text field handler add the

00:02:52.882 --> 00:02:54.428
field to the document.

00:02:54.882 --> 00:02:59.429
Keyword indexes the value as a single token, and Stored lets you read

00:02:59.429 --> 00:03:01.854
the original value from the index.

00:03:01.854 --> 00:03:05.128
Elasticsearch and Azure AI Search offer neither.

00:03:05.128 --> 00:03:06.402
Save the field.

00:03:08.049 --> 00:03:12.283
Documents are built when a record changes or a task is processed, so

00:03:12.283 --> 00:03:15.887
existing documents change only when they are built again.

00:03:15.887 --> 00:03:18.919
In the Actions menu of the index, choose Reset.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:23.221
Reset keeps the index and writes the documents again.

00:03:23.221 --> 00:03:25.695
Rebuild deletes the index first.

00:03:25.695 --> 00:03:31.620
Query Index then lists the stored fields, including a column named BlogPost.Subtitle.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:40.055
To add values for any record, implement I Document Index Handler.

00:03:40.055 --> 00:03:45.900
The Content Item Index Coordinator, which calls the part and field handlers, is one of them.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:51.247
Every handler receives every record that is indexed, so check the type of

00:03:51.247 --> 00:03:54.505
the record first, and return when it is not yours.

00:03:54.505 --> 00:03:57.880
Then call Set with a name, a value, and the options.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.689
A source is a provider name and a type.

00:04:04.689 --> 00:04:09.502
For a built-in provider, call its helper, such as Add Lucene Indexing Source,

00:04:09.502 --> 00:04:12.749
with your type, a display name, and a description.

00:04:12.749 --> 00:04:15.300
They appear when you choose Add index.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:20.730
For a provider of your own, call Add Indexing Source with three services.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:26.477
The index manager creates, rebuilds, and deletes indexes; the document index manager writes

00:04:26.477 --> 00:04:31.750
documents and keeps the last task id; the name provider builds the full index name.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:37.393
Record changes with Create Task Async on I Indexing Task Manager, giving a

00:04:37.393 --> 00:04:40.451
record id, a category, and Update or Delete.

00:04:40.451 --> 00:04:43.264
The category is the type of your source.

00:04:43.264 --> 00:04:46.200
Tasks are saved at the end of the request.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:51.674
Derive from Named Indexing Service, and pass the category as its name.

00:04:51.674 --> 00:04:57.145
Get Build Document Index Async returns the context for a task, or null to skip it.

00:04:57.145 --> 00:05:03.610
The base class never deletes documents, not even for Delete tasks; use the document index manager.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:10.506
Call Process Records For All Indexes Async from a background task, as the Indexing Worker does.

00:05:10.506 --> 00:05:14.948
An I Index Profile Handler can process the records when an index of

00:05:14.948 --> 00:05:20.060
your type is synchronized, and set the last task id to zero when one is reset.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.666
Register the three classes in your startup.

00:05:23.666 --> 00:05:28.389
The indexing service is a scoped service, the profile handler is added with

00:05:28.389 --> 00:05:34.510
Add Index Profile Handler, and the background task is registered as an I Background Task singleton.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:38.382
Check what your provider reads from the index profile.

00:05:38.382 --> 00:05:41.973
Lucene reads the name of the key field from the mappings of its

00:05:41.973 --> 00:05:45.200
profile, and only the content profile handler sets them.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.531
That is how indexing extends in code:

00:05:49.531 --> 00:05:53.760
handlers for parts, fields, and records, and a source for your own data.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.170
Thanks for watching.

